186345097 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345106 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186345107 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186417378 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573270 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1038 #edb/ref 17592186573313 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1039 "Transforming a 2x2 Matrix Into the Identity Matrix Using Row Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1041 #edb/ref 17592186115080 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115251 #edb/ref 1042 #uuid "77d78574-a17c-5fc3-8c14-3aaec7753b3c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1043 #edb/ref 17592186116136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1045 "Finding the Inverse of a 2x2 Matrix Using Row Operations" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186116133 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186048920 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1050 420.0 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115474 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "d6f7c63b-f172-5766-b56a-db8bf2a22464" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460869 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460870 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186460871 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1069 "q-43000" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1071 "Which of the following matrices are transformed into the $2\\times 2$ identity matrix after applying the row operations ${R}_{1}↔{R}_{2}\\,$ and $\\,{R}_{1}:=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1}?$\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix},\\,B=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix},\\,C=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "90c835e0-83aa-519b-9995-715569dd5d93" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186115852 #edb/ref 1074 "Let's apply the operations to each of the matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times  \\\\ B & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ 2 & 0\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =I\\,✓ \\\\ C & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & ↔{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & \\ne I\\,\\times \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186116133 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116136 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116133 #edb/ref 1061 17230 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116133 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "d8efc5ed-eaaf-514d-b03a-248df406a7d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116133 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186116498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116134 #edb/ref 1061 17231 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116134 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e874c19f-8e51-5ff3-b8d2-dd1a0540bbc2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116134 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115249 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116135 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116134 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116135 #edb/ref 1061 17232 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116135 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "7cf9141d-1c90-5675-a333-b0aa4e0f3cef" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116135 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115250 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186116135 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1061 17233 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "e2dcd832-c069-5309-a7a3-27850adf28e2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116136 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186115251 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose we want to find the inverse of the matrix \n\n![](images/fd/fd9e9dc44e27810ef490a898a7c82ae9296eaf85ac9406cf310afe86638f71e1.svg) \n\nWe can compute the inverse of $A$ using elementary row operations. There are two key advantages to using row operations to compute the inverse of a matrix:\n\n- The method works for any invertible matrix of any size, not just $2\\times 2$ matrices.\n\n- It requires significantly less computation than some alternative methods. This is especially true for large matrices.\n\nHere, we'll focus on the $2\\times 2$ case. We use the following fact:\n\n*The row operations that transform $A$ into the identity matrix $I_2$ also transform $I_2$ into $A^{-1}$*.\n\nSo, to find the inverse of $A,$ we proceed as follows:\n\n**Step 1:** Write down $\\color{blue}A$ and the identity matrix $I_2$ side-by-side, as shown below:\n![](images/e5/e553bcc265698432452c8d35b79fe9dc70c25010a0bcc6874ad0302ea7033ed7.svg)\n\n**Step 2:** Apply the standard Gauss-Jordan method to transform the matrix $A$ into the identity matrix. We perform the same row operations on both matrices:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[A\\,|\\,{I}_{2}\\,] & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 7 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :={R}_{2}+(-3){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :={R}_{1}+(-2){R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 7 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 1 & -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\,I\\,|\\,{A}^{-1}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe matrix $A^{-1}$ is obtained on the right-hand side of our big matrix after the transformation. Therefore, \n\n![](images/b7/b7c08e9a28ebfe456a9d77ccdaf6688893dfd04ba755a989a417c8a43e80ee0c.svg)\n\nWe can easily check our result by computing $AA^{-1}\\mathbin{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A{A}^{-1} & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 2 \\\\ 3 & 7\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,✓\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1065 3167 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fc38ae42-2cd4-53df-9ad5-568d8e3b5fd4" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186116498 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263934 #edb/ref 1080 "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186263934 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186263934 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "6c5e09aa-a71a-5bc1-abdb-ffbee8ae45ee" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264066 #edb/ref 1080 "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264066 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264066 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7b00896b-7411-5bea-b442-6e238235690f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264072 #edb/ref 1080 "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264072 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264072 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "7c1c69e8-11c6-5242-a605-cf735a7f46aa" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264286 #edb/ref 1080 "{A}^{-1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ 3 & -1\\end{bmatrix}" #edb/ref 17592186045443] [17592186264286 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186264286 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "967a322b-3f8a-5022-9edc-92691060c3ad" #edb/ref 1759